From 0d297241f940a59a463f66cd63c9e1aa12ef637c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akiko <akiko@macmini.lan>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:40:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(papers3): honour usbDetect polarity and report external
 power

The PaperS3's usbDetect pin (GPIO5) is driven LOW while a cable is attached,
not HIGH as the profile comment and the support doc claimed. Measured on
hardware: floating, pull-up and pull-down all read 0 with USB connected and
the battery full. Because the board has no charger IC and no gauge,
isExternalPowerPresent() had no source at all, and isCharging() goes false on
a full battery while the cable stays in, so a consumer could not tell
"plugged in, full" from "on battery".

- BoardProfile.usbDetectActiveHigh (default true, matching the documented
  "HIGH = USB present" convention, so no existing profile changes) lets a
  profile state the polarity. M5PAPER_S3 sets false and spells out the
  trailing fields it now needs.
- BatteryMonitor::isExternalPowerPresent() falls back to the detect pin when
  there is no gauge or PMIC to ask, reading it with the pull that leaves a
  disconnected pin in the absent state (pull-up for active-low, pull-down for
  active-high) so a floating line cannot report a cable that is not there.
- BatteryMonitor.cpp and its host test include <initializer_list> themselves:
  BoardConfig.h uses it in a range-for, which firmware builds got
  transitively but host builds did not.
- New host test covers the new path: active-low present/absent, the
  active-high default, and "no detect pin" still answering unknown.

Also bump m5stack/M5GFX to 0.2.33 in platformio.sample.ini and the support
doc: 0.2.20 is no longer on the PlatformIO registry (it offers 0.1.17 and
0.2.27..0.2.33), so a fresh `pio run -e papers3` stops at UnknownPackageError.

While there, record what checked out on hardware, since the doc lists these as
pending validation: panel rotation (540x960 portrait), the GT911 axes with
swapXY/flipX/flipY/gt911CoordsAtByte0 as configured (corner taps land where
they are drawn), and the x2.0 battery divider (4178 mV -> 100%).

Tested: five firmware environments build against this; the BatteryMonitor host
suite passes (54178 + 39 checks, plus the new usb-detect checks).
